295	2	@@cEternal Corridor - The Junction@@N	A junction where five passages meet, though the space is only large\nenough for three. The extra passages occupy the same physical space\nas the existing ones, overlapping without intersection. Choosing\nwhich passage to take requires understanding not just direction but\nwhich layer of reality each passage belongs to.\n	8201	11
296	2	@@cEternal Corridor - The Long Walk@@N	A straight passage that runs for what feels like miles, though the\ncitadel's exterior dimensions could not accommodate such length.\nThe passage does not curve or branch. Markers left by earlier travelers\nappear at regular intervals, counting off some distance unit. The\ncount reaches over four thousand before stopping abruptly.\n	8201	11
297	2	@@cEternal Corridor - The Wide Hall@@N	An anomaly in the corridor system: a wide, high-ceilinged hall where\nmultiple paths converge. The space feels stable compared to the\npassages around it. Several travel-worn campsites from various\neras are scattered across the floor, suggesting travelers have\nused this as a rest point. A few old campfires still show traces\nof ash.\n	8201	11
298	2	@@cEternal Corridor - The Mirrored Stretch@@N	Both walls of this passage are polished to mirror-brightness, and the\nceiling is angled to create an infinite regression of reflections. The\neffect makes it impossible to judge where the actual passage ends and\nthe reflection begins. More than one traveler has walked into a wall\nwhile staring at the ceiling.\n	8201	11
299	2	@@cEternal Corridor - The Ascending Descent@@N	A corridor that slopes both upward and downward simultaneously - an\nimpossibility that the Eternal Corridors accomplish through folded\nspace. Walking it in either direction produces the sensation of going\nuphill. Despite this, there is no actual vertical movement; anyone\nwho enters from one end exits from the same end, having somehow\ntraversed the full length without going anywhere.\n	8201	11
