964	6	@@yAncient Road Fragment@@N	@@yRemnants of an old stone road push persistently through the sand despite millennia of burial and erosion. The paving stones are massive and fitted together with extraordinary precision that speaks eloquently of a civilization now lost to time. Each stone is cut from black granite and marble, fitted so tightly that a blade cannot pass between them. The scale of the construction is impressiveâeach individual stone weighs many tons, yet they remain aligned and level after ages of exposure to the desert's harshness. The path leads toward distant ruins visible in the heat-shimmer to the south, rising gradually from the sand as if climbing toward the sky. Alongside the road, the faint outline of what once were pillars or markers suggests a grand processional way. Carved into some of the visible stones are decorative grooves and patternsâgeometric designs and occasional pictographs depicting scenes of triumph and civilization. This was once a route of prestige and power.@@N\n	32	10
965	6	@@bBazaar Entrance Ramp@@N	@@bA long ramp of precisely cut stone descends steeply into shadow and cool darkness. The entrance to the buried city lies far below, and you can feel the draw of the descent before you even commit to it. The ramp's edge is worn smooth by thousands of footsteps across countless centuries of use, each step by a merchant or pilgrim adding its wear to the eternal stone. The air grows noticeably cooler as you descend, a welcome respite from the furnace heat of the surface, smelling of deep stone dust and the irreplaceable aroma of great age. The walls alongside the ramp are carved with protective wards and inscriptions in the ancient language of Solahrâprayers for safe passage and prosperity for those who travel below. The stone beneath your feet is slightly slippery with age and mineral deposits. Darkness grows deeper with each downward step, swallowing light until you are descended into a world where the sun never reaches.@@N\n	8	11
966	6	@@bCollapsed Market Stall Row@@N	@@bRuins of what were once elegant merchant stalls lie in jumbled heaps of debris and ruin, their wooden frameworks long since rotted away to nothing. Clay jars of various sizes and broken pottery shards are scattered everywhere across the floor, fragments of an economy and way of life now utterly extinct. The pieces still bear faint marks of their original decorationâpatterns of vines and fruit, merchant marks and seals of authenticity. A faint smell of spicesânutmeg, cinnamon, myrrh, clovesâstill lingers impossibly after all these years, suggesting that some residual essence of the original merchandise lingers in the very stone itself. The shelves that once displayed wares are collapsed and broken, their wooden supports having surrendered to rot and the pressure of centuries. In places, you can still make out the faint outline of items that rested on shelvesâimpressions in the dust showing where containers once sat. This was once a place of life and commerce, animated by voices calling out wares and the negotiation of price.@@N\n	8	11
