6700	42	@@yThreshold Post Road@@N	The post road climbs north through @@r black basalt@@N step-formations, each fractured shelf worn smooth by centuries of caravan passage.  Survey markers placed by the @@yKeepers of Measure@@N stand at irregular intervals, their condition-window notations nearly consumed by ash accumulation and lichen.  Behind you, the @@gcinderpine tree-line@@N of the lower forest presses close to the verge; ahead, the road broadens toward a collapsed administrative post that once marked the boundary between civic jurisdiction and mountain route-house authority.  Old caravan ruts are still legible in the compacted volcanic aggregate beside the paving, and tally stones near the east verge bear three generations of overwritten conversion marks.  The air carries a faint mineral sweetness — volcanic rock warming in morning sun — and the unmistakable quality of altitude beginning.\n	0	4
6701	42	@@yThreshold Post Ruins@@N	The Threshold Post was once the administrative junction where mountain route-house governance ended and lowland civic authority began — a boundary seven times contested in formal legal proceedings.  What remains is a collapsed stone building of substantial construction, its walls cracked by frost and volcanic tremor, its interior exposed to sky where the roof fell in during an Ashfall-era ash-load event.  @@yWarden commission debris@@N covers the central platform: ledger fragments, overwritten schedule boards, a single iron signpost still bearing faded route designations for three passes.  The hub opens onto five distinct approaches, each heading toward a different regime of the mountain's interior, and experienced travelers stop here to orient before committing to any direction.  The @@r basalt footholds@@N and the chalk-ash traces of the Chalkwind Saddle approaches are both visible from this position.\n	4	5
6702	42	@@ySalvage Assessment House Forecourt@@N	The forecourt of the Salvage Assessment House retains its formal layout despite decades of abandonment: a flagstoned entry yard, two crumbling pillars that once bore hanging seals of commercial authority, and a notice post where contested authority declarations are still nailed in overlapping layers.  The @@yKiess-funded Assessment House@@N operated here as a certification point for volcanic materials extracted from the upper range — mirror-quality obsidian, sulfur blooms, and pumice each required separate origin documentation before they could enter the lowland trade network.  The newest notice is older than any living warden, its ink faded to illegibility; the oldest is only a pressed impression in the wood where iron letters once spelled a Compact certification requirement.\n	0	4
6703	42	@@yAssessment House Interior@@N	Inside the Assessment House, archive shelves line three walls from floor to collapsed ceiling, their contents reduced to compressed debris: ledger spines, clay seal fragments, and the occasional iron clasp from a document case.  The sorting tables in the center were built for the authentication of volcanic trade materials — obsidian, pumice, sulfur crystals — and their surfaces still bear stain patterns from decades of classification work.  A @@yCompact certification placard@@N is mounted near the south wall, its text specifying the origin documentation required for each grade of Cinderteeth material; the placard survived because it was cast in @@r iron@@N rather than written on paper.  The vault passage at the west end is marked by a heavy stone doorframe, the vault door hanging open on rusted pivot pins.\n	8	11
6704	42	@@yAssessment House Vault Room@@N	The vault is empty.  It was emptied before the post was abandoned — the relic-classification plaques fixed to the inner walls testify to this: CURRENT HOLDINGS CERTIFIED, they read in stamped metal, but the hooks and shelves beneath them hold nothing.  The plaques themselves were too heavy to remove easily and too specific to be useful elsewhere, and so they remain: a catalog of what was once held and formally extracted before whatever final authority pulled out last.  The vault walls are dressed limestone rather than the basalt used throughout the rest of the structure, a deliberate choice to prevent thermal contamination of sensitive material classifications.\n	12	11
6705	42	@@yBandit Clearance Post Alpha@@N	The Bandit Clearance Post was built to provide armed oversight of the canyon approaches after early caravan masters complained that Ashfang Remnant activity was cutting into convoy schedules.  The fieldstone walls are thicker than strictly necessary — a tell of a building designed to withstand siege rather than merely shelter a patrol — and the interior shutters on the western window slots still close.  Bloodstained sections of the east wall record a succession of violent incidents, the stains in layers of different age.  Old warden rotation logs are pinned above the entry: four-week shifts, three wardens per rotation, last entry more than a generation ago.\n	0	4
6706	42	@@yBandit Clearance Post Alpha — Inner Room@@N	The post's inner room served as a combined armory and sleeping quarters, the separation between martial readiness and rest dissolved by the demands of isolated postings.  Weapon racks line the south wall; every rack is stripped bare, the weapons taken in the orderly fashion of a planned withdrawal rather than abandoned in a rout.  Sleeping pallets folded against the north wall still hold the impressions of occupation.  A fragment of the old warden commission charter is nailed above the weapons racks: it specifies the patrol territory, the mob's rules of engagement, and the quarterly resupply schedule, the last line of which has been crossed out and replaced with the words "SUSPENDED — AUTHORITY UNRESOLVED."\n	8	11
6707	42	@@yBlackstep Lower Road — North Fork@@N	The north fork junction is marked by a cracked basalt milestone whose inscription reads only THE SADDLES in weathered script, direction being understood by anyone traveling this road with purpose.  The @@r black basalt foothold@@N step-formations become more pronounced here, each ledge a natural stair that the mountain's thermal cycling has fractured and re-set over geological time.  The fork splits deliberately: the main north road climbs toward the saddle passes; a cinderpine track branches west; and the ridge country opens east toward the obsidian fan approaches.  Ash accumulation in the low points between the footholds suggests recent wind activity from the upper range.\n	0	4
6829	42	@@rSaddle Eastern Junction@@N	The eastern junction of the northern saddle arc connects the traverse route from the outer ridgeline to the southern approach corridor below.  @@r Dark basalt@@N dominates the surface here — the junction sits at the boundary between the chalk-ash saddle chemistry below and the volcanic geology above, and the volcanic character is clearly winning at this elevation.  From this junction, the southern approach corridor descends toward the Chalkwind Convergence zone and the main route into the Bellspine territory beyond.\n	0	5
6708	42	@@yBlackstep Lower Road — East Fork@@N	The east fork leaves the main north road and descends toward the fan country, the basalt underfoot giving way to obsidian aggregate where old lava flows have been worn down by caravan traffic.  Early outcrops of dark volcanic glass begin here — tool-quality material, not the mirror-grade of the upper fans, but recognizable enough that trained eyes pause to assess.  The ridge country visible to the north carries the distinctive scorch-patterning of old pyroclastic activity, and the cinderpine cover thins as the terrain becomes more openly volcanic.  A secondary climb path switchbacks upward to the east, used by survey parties reaching the old Keeper marker station above.\n	0	4
6709	42	@@rBlack Basalt Ridge@@N	The ridge crest exposes a stratum of @@r jet-black basalt@@N fractured into irregular columns by centuries of thermal cycling — the mountain's equivalent of a structural memory, each crack a record of a cycle the rock survived and reset.  The ashrunner warrens are visible in the shallow depressions between columns: tunnel openings the diameter of a cart wheel, their rims worn smooth by generations of passage, the substrate around them compacted and stained from years of nest-warming activity.  Sight lines from this position cover most of the lower foothills, and experienced travelers use the ridge as a waypoint for identifying weather and ash-column activity in the upper zones.  The terrain drops south toward the east fork road and north toward the higher ridges above.\n	0	5
6710	42	@@gCinderpine Copse@@N	A dense stand of @@g dark-barked cinderpines@@N clusters in the hollow between two old lava flow margins, the trees growing in a formation that reflects the substrate beneath: where the lava stopped, the cinderpines began.  The resin of these trees carries a faint sulfur note that distinguishes them from lowland conifers, a chemical adaptation to the volcanic soil chemistry visible in the orange-tinged sap beading on trunk scars where branches have been harvested.  Resin harvest marks — shallow blade cuts with collection notches below — cover most accessible trunk surfaces within arm's reach.  The canopy here creates a rare pocket of shade on the otherwise exposed lower slopes.\n	0	4
6711	42	@@rAshrunner Warren Flats@@N	The flat ash-deposit terrain here is honeycombed with ashrunner burrow openings, each entrance a collapsed ring of compacted substrate where thousands of exits and entrances have worn the ground-crust thin.  The surface has a hollow sound underfoot in the sections between major warren clusters — a result of the lateral tunnel networks connecting individual burrow systems into a community infrastructure capable of supporting colonies measured in hundreds.  Faint movement is visible at most warren entrances when a figure stands still long enough: the ashrunners are aware of intruders long before any intruder is aware of them.  The terrain is more stable to the north where natural rock outcrops interrupt the burrowed substrate.\n	0	4
6712	42	@@rScorched Pine Ridge@@N	A line of charred cinderpine remnants marks the edge of an old lahar flow that stripped the eastern face of this ridge and left the surviving trees at its margin bleached white from heat exposure.  The lahar did not reach far enough to kill these pines outright — they were scorched rather than burned, and the subsequent regrowth around their bleached trunks creates a visual record of thermal injury and recovery that Keeper survey crews noted as a visible stratigraphy of eruptive events.  From this ridge, the Assessment House ruins are visible below and to the southwest, and the geometry of the lower foothold approaches becomes legible as a system rather than a tangle.  The rock underfoot is particularly stable here — the same thermal event that bleached the pines baked the substrate into a dense ceramic layer.\n	0	5
6713	42	@@yThermal Seep Ground@@N	A warm-water seep emerges here through a fracture in the basalt bedrock, staining the rock face orange with iron mineral deposits that have built up over geological time into formations resembling arrested rust.  @@G Sulfur moss@@N — a thermophilic ground cover that indicates active geothermal conditions — carpets the wet margins of the seep in a vivid biological contrast to the dark volcanic substrate.  The water temperature is noticeably elevated compared to the surrounding runoff: warm enough to produce faint steam on cold mornings, warm enough that the ground immediately surrounding the seep never freezes.  The iron-sulfur chemistry of the water makes it undrinkable without filtration, a fact that the absence of animal tracks near the water confirms.\n	0	2
6714	42	@@rVolcanic Basalt Stairway@@N	A natural stairway of step-fractured @@r basalt@@N rises through the central ridge mass here, each tread a horizontal face of rock that the mountain's own thermal expansion and contraction has separated from the vertical mass above and below.  This geological accident of fracture geometry has been worn smoother than any manufactured stair by generations of traffic — survey crews, caravan scouts, and route wardens all used this as the primary access route to the upper volcanic terrain.  The Threshold Post hub is visible below through a gap in the ridge; above, the stairway continues into the scorched pine terrain of the upper approaches.  The resonance of the mountain is first perceptible here as a faint pressure through the boot soles at certain times — a precursor to the much stronger Bell phenomenon of the upper zones.\n	0	5
6715	42	@@yOld Survey Marker Station@@N	A collapsed Keeper survey post occupies a natural rock shelf on the ridge, its stone baseline marker — a rectangular block of dressed limestone distinct from the surrounding volcanic basalt — still standing at the shelf's edge where it was placed to provide a fixed reference point for triangulation surveys of the volcanic arc.  The marker's carved face specifies a baseline designation, a measurement date in Keeper notation, and a condition-window code whose meaning is no longer standard in any current survey vocabulary.  The wooden superstructure of the post has long since collapsed into a pile of weathered timber, leaving only the stone marker and the iron anchor bolts that once secured the instrument mounting.  The site retains utility as an observation point — the sight line alignment the Keepers chose for this marker provides an excellent view of the major fan approaches to the east.\n	0	5
6716	42	@@yFoothills Canyon Mouth@@N	The canyon mouth narrows the broad foothill terrain into a choke-point where @@r basalt walls@@N rise on both sides to heights that block the sun at midday.  The transition is abrupt — the open slope of the lower approaches ends at a ragged line where the canyon's geological fault first breached the surface, and within a few steps the sky narrows to a strip of light between the wall crests.  Ashfang Remnant sentry positions are visible in carved ledge-notches on both walls above — not high enough to be inaccessible, but high enough to give a clear advantage to anyone defending from above.  The path continues north into the canyon's interior where the sound of wind drops to nothing and the volcanic chemistry of the enclosed space becomes sharply noticeable.\n	0	5
6717	42	@@rCanyon West Wall@@N	The west wall of the canyon is sheer @@r black basalt@@N from the floor to the top of the cliff line, with the exception of a narrow ledge system at mid-height where water seepage has eroded a horizontal band of softer material.  The ledge is wide enough to walk if you know where to step and narrow enough to be dangerous if you do not.  Old carved footholds in the lower wall face — Ashfang work, cut with the practical efficiency of people who needed the height regularly — lead to the ledge system from the canyon floor.  From the ledge, the full length of the canyon is visible and the positions of the inner camp are clearly readable.\n	0	5
6718	42	@@rCanyon East Wall@@N	The east wall of the canyon bears a different character than the west: the rock face has been tagged from floor to accessible height with Ashfang Remnant territorial markings — gang sigils, operational tallies, and the occasional crude map-fragment scratched into the basalt with iron points.  Most of the markings overlap in layers, the older ones partially obscured by newer work, creating a sediment-record of the Remnant's organizational history at this site.  A few marks appear to be in a pre-Ashfang script — possibly old warden notation that predates the Remnant's occupation — visible in the lowest, most weathered layer.\n	0	5
6719	42	@@rCanyon Floor — Patrol Zone@@N	The canyon floor is wide enough for a column of four to walk abreast, the basalt underfoot worn to a slick surface by the foot traffic of occupying Ashfang Remnant patrols.  The enclosed acoustics amplify every sound — footsteps, voices, and the distant hum of activity from the camps to the north — making it impossible to approach quietly and equally difficult to identify the direction of any sound heard within the canyon.  Ashfang patrol circuits are visible as worn-path patterns in the canyon floor, their geometry documenting the territory that is actively defended versus the edges that are merely claimed.  The walls to east and west offer elevated observation positions but no easy escape routes once inside the canyon's main axis.\n	0	5
6720	42	@@rAshfang Remnant Outer Camp@@N	The outer camp sprawls across the widest section of the canyon floor, a dense arrangement of salvaged canvas shelters, cook-fire rings, and supply caches that reveals the Ashfang Remnant's genuine settlement ambitions — this is not a temporary position.  The shelters are organized in the radial pattern of a defended encampment: a central fire ring surrounded by sleeping shelters, supply caches to the east wall, and a weapons-staging area to the west with clear sight lines to both the southern canyon approach and the northern tollbridge.  Cooking smells, sulfur-infused smoke from the canyon rock, and the ambient noise of a population living at close quarters make the outer camp a densely sensory environment.  The Ashfang personnel here are not raiders in transit — they are occupiers with supply lines, organizational hierarchy, and territorial confidence.\n	0	5
