7290	42	@@rFirsttooth Lower Caldera — Western Alcove@@N	The western alcove off the lower caldera face is a sheltered indent in the caldera wall — @@r the alcove geometry@@N deflects the thermal updraft around the opening, creating a pocket where the ambient temperature is measurably lower than the exposed caldera face immediately adjacent.  Commission survey records note the alcove as a rest point during lower caldera survey traversals, its sheltered character making it the most survivable fixed position on the lower caldera face during high-emission events.  The lower caldera face connects east and the approach continues west with a subsurface vent below.\n	32	5
7291	42	@@rFirsttooth Lower Caldera — Western Approach@@N	The western approach to the lower caldera is the outermost extent of the caldera face accessible from the western alcove — @@r the western approach terrain@@N shows more weathered geological character than the main caldera face, its position on the caldera's lee side reducing the direct thermal impact and allowing older surface features to persist without the repeated resurfacing that affects the more exposed portions of the caldera.  Commission survey records document this approach as providing geological access to caldera-wall strata that are not exposed on the main face.  The western alcove connects east.\n	32	5
7292	42	@@rFirsttooth Caldera — Lower Subsurface Vent@@N	The lower subsurface vent is accessible through a floor opening in the western alcove above — @@r the vent passage@@N descends a short distance into the caldera's outer rock structure, its walls showing the characteristic gas-condensation deposits of a secondary vent connected to the caldera system's pressure-release network.  Commission geological notes for this vent document its emission chemistry and emission cycle, both consistent with the broader Firsttooth caldera system.  The western alcove connects above.\n	32	5
7293	42	@@rSecondtooth Approach — Western Shoulder@@N	The western shoulder of the Secondtooth approach ridge is the lateral extension of the ridge connecting Firsttooth and Secondtooth to the west — @@r the shoulder terrain@@N is geologically distinct from the main ridge, its surface exposure on the western face receiving different weathering conditions that have produced a scoria surface with more rounded features than the wind-stripped main ridge.  Commission survey records note the western shoulder as the observation point for the caldera's western exterior face, its position providing access to geological features not visible from the main ridge traverse.  The approach ridge connects east and the far western face is accessible.\n	32	5
7294	42	@@rSecondtooth — Western Caldera Face@@N	The western face of the Secondtooth caldera is the geologically quieter face, its position away from the prevailing thermal output orientation meaning that the surface features here have accumulated over more eruptive cycles without resurfacing — @@r the western face scoria@@N shows layering and color banding from multiple eruption cycles that the more active eastern and northern faces do not preserve.  Commission survey records document the western face as providing the longest-duration geological record accessible on the Secondtooth, its preserved stratigraphy extending the eruptive history documentation beyond what single-cycle surface geology allows.  The western shoulder connects east.\n	32	5
7295	42	@@rSecondtooth — Western Fumarole Pocket@@N	The western fumarole pocket below the Secondtooth approach ridge's western shoulder is a gas-accumulation depression in the caldera's outer structure — @@r the pocket's chemical profile@@N differs from the eastern fumarole field: the Secondtooth's geological coupling with the Firsttooth produces a mixed gas signature at this western position that the commission's gas-monitoring team found diagnostically significant for distinguishing the two caldera systems' individual contributions to the shared vent network.  Commission survey records document this pocket as a key measurement point for the coupled caldera gas-chemistry model.  The western shoulder connects above.\n	32	5
7296	42	@@REmber Throne — Eastern Witness Chamber@@N	The eastern witness chamber adjacent to the Ember Throne is the space where those who have survived the encounter with the Ember Throne Patriarch have traditionally recorded their testimony — @@R the chamber walls@@N are covered in carved accounts from multiple cultural traditions, the records of those who stood before the Throne and returned, their inscriptions constituting the most concentrated archive of first-person encounter testimony in the Cinderteeth range.  Commission records note that the witness chamber documentation spans multiple centuries of accounts, with older accounts in scripts the commission's epigraphic observer could not fully translate and recent accounts in the regional common script legible to commission team members.  The Ember Throne connects west and the northern witness alcove extends above.\n	44	11
7297	42	@@RCaldera Interior — Upper Witness Alcove@@N	The upper witness alcove within the caldera interior is an elevated position accessible from the eastern witness chamber — @@R the alcove's elevated position@@N provides a view down into the Ember Throne chamber that the floor-level chamber does not offer, a perspective that the commission's cultural observer noted as consistently described in the oldest witness testimonies as the position from which the Throne's full geological scale is most apparent.  Commission survey records document the upper alcove as the position used by the survey team's cultural observer during the Ember Throne documentation phase, allowing simultaneous observation of the Throne and the witness chamber testimonies.  The witness chamber connects south and the northern caldera extension leads east.\n	44	11
7298	42	@@RCaldera Interior — Northern Extension@@N	The northern extension of the Ember Throne's caldera interior is the furthest accessible point within the caldera structure in the northern direction — @@R the extension terrain@@N is characterized by the densest mineral crystallization in the caldera interior, the thermal gradient at this position favoring the deposition of the caldera's mineral load at a rate that the commission's mineralogical observer described as the highest direct crystallization rate documented in the range.  Commission survey records for this extension are primarily mineralogical, focused on the crystal-type identification and deposition-rate documentation that the unusual conditions here make possible.  The upper witness alcove connects west and the summit witness point extends north.\n	44	11
7299	42	@@RCaldera Summit Witness Point@@N	The caldera summit witness point is the highest accessible position within the Firsttooth caldera's interior structure — @@R the summit witness point@@N is where the caldera's geological forces are most concentrated: maximum temperature, maximum mineral crystallization rate, maximum gas concentration, and the closest proximity to the caldera ceiling that the geological structure permits.  Commission survey records designate this position as the absolute limit of interior survey penetration, its measurements representing the survey team's greatest sustained exposure to the caldera's extreme environment.  The Ash Cult's doctrinal records treat this position as the innermost sanctum of the Ember Crown — the point of greatest proximity to the geological intelligence they document as the Ember Throne Patriarch.  The northern extension connects south.\n	44	11
