7800	44	@@yThe Guard Command Hall@@N	A large, well-marked building near @@WMidgaard's@@N central square. Stone walls bear the Guard Command's banner — a crossed sword and lantern on grey field. The main hall is spacious and orderly, with weapon racks along the walls and a duty board near the entrance. An enrollment desk sits prominently near the door, staffed by an officer ready to process new recruits.\n\n	0	0
7900	44	@@yThe Wall Command Garrison@@N	A stone fortress near @@WKiess's@@N frontier gate. The garrison's thick walls are functional rather than decorative, built to withstand siege rather than impress visitors. Maps of frontier patrol routes cover every available wall surface, and a large sand table in the center shows current deployments. A recruitment post stands near the main entrance.\n\n	0	0
8000	44	@@yThe Harbor Syndics Hall@@N	A large administrative complex on @@WKowloon's@@N central wharf. The smell of salt air and cargo tar mingles with ink and parchment. Stacked ledgers line the shelves, trade manifests cover the desks, and a large chart of Kowloon's delta port dominates the far wall. A registration desk near the entrance processes new members.\n\n	0	0
8100	44	@@yThe Shrine of the First Claw@@N	A stone-and-bronze temple complex near @@WMafdet's@@N central market. Bronze claw sigils are set into the floor at the threshold, and the dim interior is lit by oil lamps that cast long shadows across the judgment benches. A petitioner's desk near the entrance handles enrollment for those who would serve the Shrine's oath-enforcement mission.\n\n	0	0
8200	44	@@yThe Ember Warden Station@@N	A fortified compound near @@WRakuen's@@N main gate. Emergency equipment hangs from pegs on every wall — rope coils, fire blankets, medical kits, signal lanterns. The station has the purposeful clutter of a working emergency response center. A muster desk near the entrance processes new volunteers.\n\n	0	0
8300	44	@@yThe Road Warden Lodge@@N	A traveler's compound at the @@WMidgaard@@N crossroads, where the major routes to Kowloon, Kiess, Rakuen, and Mafdet diverge. The lodge smells of leather, trail rations, and campfire smoke. Maps of every major trade route cover the walls, and a large route board tracks active patrols. A charter desk near the entrance accepts new members.\n\n	0	0
8310	44	@@yRoad Warden Waystation@@N	A small but well-maintained outpost beside @@WKowloon's@@N Lantern Gate. A route board displays the current patrol schedule between Kowloon and the inland cities, and a lockbox of emergency supplies sits beneath the window. The waystation smells of road dust and oiled leather. A charter desk handles local business for wardens passing through.\n\n	0	0
8311	44	@@yRoad Warden Waystation@@N	A frontier waystation built above @@WKiess's@@N North Gate staging yard. From the elevated post, the road north toward the Forest of Confusion is visible through a narrow observation window. Route schedules, caravan manifests, and patrol reports are pinned to a corkboard wall. The waystation doubles as a rest stop for wardens transitioning between frontier and city assignments.\n\n	0	0
8312	44	@@yRoad Warden Waystation@@N	A compact waystation at the foot of @@WRakuen's@@N first terrace stairs, positioned where arriving travelers gather to consult the route boards. Maps of the terrace approach roads and causeway conditions cover the walls. The station's open-front design allows wardens to observe foot traffic while processing paperwork at the charter desk.\n\n	0	0
8313	44	@@yRoad Warden Waystation@@N	A waystation at the eastern junction of @@WMafdet's@@N Carter's Way, overlooking the approach to the harbor and the Ledger Quarter. Caravan route charts specific to the coastal roads are posted alongside standard patrol schedules. The station handles warden business for the port city's considerable overland trade traffic.\n\n	0	0
8320	44	@@yHarbor Syndics Trade Liaison Office@@N	A basement office beneath @@WMafdet's@@N Market of the Shoreward Tables, accessible by a worn stone staircase. The Syndics maintain this satellite office to coordinate trade documentation between Kowloon's port authority and Mafdet's market compact. Stacked cargo manifests, tariff schedules, and harbor clearance forms cover every surface. A clerk processes cross-port trade filings at a desk surrounded by filing cabinets.\n\n	0	0
