141	18	1	0	The Confluence Texts are the primary scholarly legacy of the Chromatic Circle—vast libraries\ndocumenting every observed interaction between magical spell schools that Circle practitioners\ncould produce, test, and reproduce. Unlike the Axiomatic Schools' formal proofs, which were\norganized by individual school, the Confluence Texts were organized by interaction type:\nresonance effects, interference effects, amplification effects, cancellation effects, and the\ncomplex multi-party effects that emerge when practitioners from different schools engage\nsimultaneously. This organizational structure was deliberate and controversial. The Axiomatic\nSchools classified much of the Texts' content as unverifiable—not because Circle practitioners\ndocumented incorrectly but because verification required multi-school simultaneous engagement\nthat single-school practitioners structurally could not perform. The Circle's response was\ntechnically correct and institutionally ineffective: institutions controlling credential\nrecognition and research funding defined the scope of legitimate inquiry, and the Texts fell\noutside that scope. During academic consolidation, the Confluent Theorists—the Circle faction\nthat maintained the full theoretical implications of the Texts—produced the Convergence Codices,\nlargely suppressed. The Texts survive in fragmented form across apprentice chains, each\npreserving the sections most relevant to the elemental schools its practitioners specialized in.
