e supposed to contain the dead now sustain the wetland ecology that replaced ordered governance.\nShadowmere is a frontier mirror of Midgaard's own institutional tensions: record supremacy, containment bureaucracy, emergency drift, and memory conflict all appear here in harsher and more consequential form.\nEvery faction invokes law. None can establish uncontested legitimacy. Contradictory records in Shadowmere can all be technically valid yet mutually destructive.\nArchitecture progresses as procedure: intake to classification to confinement to transformation to disposal to appeal denied.\nFor Midgaard scholars, Shadowmere is a warning archive — proof that lawful systems become predatory when emergency powers lose limits.
