471	59	1	0	The Witness Freshness Rule is a mountain legal standard holding that route records older than one active season are treated as rumor until re-witnessed by a qualified observer.\nThe rule emerged from the Cinderteeth and adjacent mountain-pass legal tradition, where seasonal conditions cause routes to change substantially between traversal periods.\nA route record from spring traversal is not valid evidence for autumn route conditions, even along the same path — the rule requires re-attestation before any operational decision is made from old records.\nThe "active season" threshold is defined as the period since the last comparable seasonal conditions: a winter record is valid through the following winter, but requires re-attestation before the next spring route decision.\nRe-witnessing requires a qualified observer who has traversed the route under current conditions and can attest to specific condition parameters matching or deviating from the prior record.\nThe rule's origin is practical: several route failures in the Cinderteeth approach involved decisions made from outdated survey records that were treated as current evidence.\nSurveyor Sevan's condition-window methodology is the systematic form of the same principle the Witness Freshness Rule embeds in mountain legal practice.\nThe rule was adopted into Kiess's evidence-freshness standards for Syndic Council deliberations, where any route assessment older than one season requires re-attestation before serving as basis for policy.\nThe mountain legal tradition distinguishes between "fresh witness" (direct observation this season), "aged witness" (prior season, requires re-attestation), and "historical record" (two or more seasons past, admissible only as background context).\nAt the interface with lowland law, the Witness Freshness Rule creates friction: lowland systems treat survey records as persistently valid until formally revoked, while mountain law treats un-re-witnessed records as automatically