501	61	6	16	Mafdet's Quarantine Ledger maintains the most extensive current documentation of Archive Schism provenance for relic and document classification. The Schism-origin classification in Mafdet's Ledger Houses means: document requires independent corroboration before legal use. The Shoreward Assessors' training includes identifying Schism-era materials by physical characteristics: specific ink compositions, seal formats, and institutional markers that identify each faction's production. Mafdet position: the Schism's legacy is active. Schism-era documents continue to circulate and be used in legal proceedings; the Quarantine Ledger classification's enhanced scrutiny remains necessary.
502	61	7	65	Khenari descent-records from the Archive Schism period are among the few external records that document the Schism's mortuary effects. Deaths during the violent phase were recorded differently by each faction: Embalmer House records and Seal College records for the same deaths contradict each other on cause, authority, and outstanding obligations. Khenari descent-record practitioners face a unique challenge: both versions of a given death record may be authentic productions of legitimate institutions, yet they contradict each other. Khenari practice is to maintain both records in parallel, note the contradiction, and flag all derived legal claims as conditionally valid pending resolution. The conditions for resolution do not currently exist: neither faction can be compelled to reconcile; no neutral authority has standing to impose reconciliation. Khenari position: the Schism did not destroy the records. It destroyed the records' claim to authority. Those are different problems requiring different solutions.
503	61	8	513	The Umbral's shadow-archives beneath Midgaard contain non-Solar-Court records of the Archive Schism period: observations made by Umbral archivists not embedded in either faction's record-keeping structure. These records are the closest thing to neutral documentation of the Schism available. Umbral shadow-archive records from the period document primarily what was absent: which administrative functions stopped operating, which locations were evacuated, which record types disappeared from circulation. The Umbral's observational mode — noting absence before presence — produced a record that neither faction's documentation captures. Umbral in Midgaard have made these records available to the Violet Compact for provenance review; the Compact uses them as calibration material for assessing Schism-era document authenticity. Umbral position: both factions kept records of what they did. Neither kept records of what they stopped doing. The shadow-archives kept records of the stopping.
504	62	1	0	The Plague Mandate was Shadowmere's emergency legal regime that expanded containment powers without reliable sunset controls.\nIt was issued jointly by Midgaard's Crown and regional lords during blight-fever and battlefield contagion outbreaks in the frontier march.\nThe Mandate gave Shadowmere's command structure authority to: impose rapid intake without lineage review, enforce forced quarantines, order immediate cremation without appeal, and temporarily suspend lineage-inheritance claims.\nEach power was justified by a specific crisis need: rapid intake managed volume, forced quarantine contained spread, immediate cremation prevented reanimation, suspension of lineage claims cleared administrative backlogs.\nTemporary was written into the Mandate's language. The definition of temporary was not written in.\nWhen plague peaks receded, no authority agreed on what rollback looked like or who had standing to declare it completed.\nCrown envoys demanded restored noble precedence; Compact wardens insisted containment powers remain; temple delegates sought release rites for mass graves; march commanders refused to relinquish emergency command chains.\nThe Mandate's powers did not expire; they calcified into permanent institutional function embedded in Shadowmere's administration.\nThe processing halls, poison works, deep binding cells, and intake trenches built during the Mandate period became permanent architecture rather than crisis infrastructure.\nShadowmere's legal identity in current governance is the Plague Mandate period's unresolved administrative state: a frontier march still operating under emergency powers that no authority has successfully rolled back.\nThe most enduring institutional product of the Mandate is the Binder Collegium, whose record-soul classification practices were originally Mandate-period intake procedures.
505	62	2	1	Midgaard's registry includes the Plague Mandate as an active open file: issued, never formally closed, legally disputed across three generations of Charter Review Board sessions.\nCurrent Midgaard governance holds that temporary powers expired by implication when crisis conditions ended; Shadowmere's command structure holds that rollback requires explicit authority, not implied expiration.\nThe Continuity faction argues emergency powers expire with the emergency, regardless of formal rescission. The Reckoning faction argues the Mandate's lack of a sunset condition is itself a policy failure for which Midgaard is responsible.\nBoth factions agree the Mandate created the problem. They disagree about whether Midgaard must acknowledge responsibility before attempting rollback.\nMidgaard cannot engage with Shadowmere without either implicitly recognizing the Mandate's ongoing force or explicitly contesting it. The file has been reviewed seventeen times and remains open.
506	62	3	4	Kowloon's Black Ledger contains the Plague Mandate as the definitive case study for emergency power failure: correct response to a real crisis, incorrect design producing permanent crisis infrastructure.\nThe Neon Covenant's crisis protocol sunset requirements were directly inspired by the Mandate's failure: every emergency power includes a defined metric triggering automatic review.\nReview is not rescission: the power does not expire at review, but the review establishes whether the crisis condition still exists and whether the power remains proportionate.\nThe Mandate had no review mechanism; powers were issued and no one was assigned to evaluate whether they remained necessary.\nKowloon's assessment: the Mandate's drafters focused on the immediate crisis and assumed someone else would handle the transition. No one did.\nThe Neon Covenant designates a transition officer with irrevocable authority to initiate rollback when conditions permit. The Mandate's failure produced this requirement.
507	62	4	2	Kiess's anti-secrecy doctrine treats the Plague Mandate as the most damaging example of emergency power without transparency requirements in the corridor's documented history.\nThe Mandate's scope was not publicly disclosed at issuance: specific powers were contained in administrative annexes the affected population could not access.\nIf the people living under the Mandate had known its full scope — including suspension of lineage-inheritance claims — they could have organized legal challenges before those challenges became impossible.\nKiess's three-power charter requires that any emergency power be publicly disclosed in full at issuance, with scope, triggering conditions, and review mechanism all documented and available.\nKiess's analysis: transparency is necessary but not sufficient — the Mandate could have been fully disclosed and still calcified if the review mechanism was inadequate. Both disclosure and sunset are necessary independently. The Mandate failed both.
508	62	5	8	Rakuen's Garden Ledger treats the Plague Mandate as the foundational document for understanding what disaster-recovery protocols must prevent.\nThe Mandate responded to a genuine crisis efficiently. When the crisis ended, the processing infrastructure had no transition to peacetime function. It continued operating. Infrastructure with no peacetime function must be decommissioned when the crisis ends; the Mandate's was not. Shadowmere is the result.\nRakuen's drawdown protocols specify this transition: when crisis metrics fall below threshold, crisis infrastructure begins managed decommission or repurposing.\nPost-crisis assessment reviews infrastructure purpose: does this serve the recovery, or does it serve the crisis that is now over?\nThis rule has been applied fourteen times. It has produced no Shadowmere outcomes. Rakuen considers this evidence that the rule works.
509	62	6	16	Mafdet's Shrine of the First Claw maintains the Plague Mandate in its oath-violation case files with an unusual classification: the Mandate itself was not an oath violation, but its continued application after the emergency ended was.\nOfficers who continued applying cremation orders to non-plague dead and using intake authority for political detention had not technically violated the Mandate — they had violated the conditions under which the Mandate's authority was legitimate.\nThe Shrine's classification: continued application of Mandate powers after the crisis ended constitutes sustained oath violation by the officers applying it, distinct from the legitimate Mandate itself.\nThe Claw Mark Registry notes: three ships' captains from the Mandate period appear in the Registry with oath-violation notations for invoking Mandate authority at Mafdet harbor after the crisis period. All three were banned from Mafdet's trade network. Their bans remain active.
510	62	7	257	Ashborn communities near Shadowmere's frontier zone were directly affected by the Plague Mandate's suspension of lineage-inheritance claims, applied without distinction between community types.\nPlague clearance required documentation that Ashborn communities, which did not use Midgaard's standard documentary practices, could not easily produce.\nThe suspension lasted longer for Ashborn communities; the clearance pathway was not designed for non-standard documentation practices.\nThis asymmetric burden was not intentional but was not corrected when the disparity became apparent. Equal application of emergency powers to communities with unequal compliance access is not equal treatment.\nAshborn communities named this problem to intake officers; the officers forwarded complaints through channels leading to the same administrators who had applied the powers.\nThe Mandate's complaint mechanism was not independent of its administration. The lesson persists in Ashborn engagement with Midgaard's administrative systems.
511	63	1	0	The Umbral are shadow given permanent form — humanoids whose substance is as much darkness as flesh, whose origin lies in the world's deepest rupture between light and anti-light. Not demons, not undead, not evil: the living residue of the Black Sun Shard's most intense influence, people who dwelt in shadow-charged spaces where the Shard's anti-light leaks into the world, whose bodies over generations incorporated anti-light into cellular foundation. The Gloamvault beneath Midgaard is their most significant habitation site, but Umbral communities exist wherever Shard influence concentrates. Physical description: difficult to perceive clearly even in direct light — the eye slides off them slightly, as though not quite fully where they appear to be. In daylight: lean, pale-gray humanoids with very dark eyes (pupil and iris sometimes indistinguishable) and hair from deep charcoal to void-black. Skin has faintly iridescent quality — simultaneously present and absent. In low light, outlines soften; in shadow, extraordinarily difficult to see. Lighter than apparent size; physical impacts that should produce specific tactile feedback sometimes produce the sensation of pressing into something that gives unexpectedly. Heal slowly in direct sunlight; recover better in darkness or shadow. Stat profile: STR 10, INT 22, WIS 14, DEX 22, CON 16. Strong in Shadow and Mental; weak in Holy and Physical; resistant to Shadow and Mental; susceptible to Holy and Fire. Racial skills: sneak, mystical potency, enhanced critical. Sneak is passive, not learned: shadow-constituted nature makes them naturally difficult to perceive. The Gloamvault predates Midgaard's First Stone Accord — the archive was already old when Midgaard was built above it. The Umbral were there first. Principle of necessary absence: what is not present shapes reality as powerfully as what is. Shadow-archives maintained in near-total darkness using phosphorescent ink.
