521	64	3	2	Kiess's charter doctrine treats the Night of Unsounded Bells as the canonical case for anti-sealed-warrant requirements. The reform bloc's failure: they had the evidence but no mechanism to compel public disclosure under the Kel'Shadra governance system. If that system had included Kiess's three-power disclosure requirement, the sealed transfer books would have been reviewable before the Night. The Night is the foundational case in Kiess's charter education: how a governance system that can classify its own abuses cannot be reformed from within. Kiess's frontier watch specifically looks for early indicators: institutions that hold both enforcement authority and record-sealing authority with no external review mechanism. Kiess's position: the Night was not a failure of good people. It was a success of a system designed to suppress exactly what the reform bloc attempted.
