546	67	3	4	Kowloon's Black Ledger references the Oathbroken Cohorts in its section on crisis-period oath failures; custody obligations are specified in writing in Neon Covenant crisis protocol, not assumed from institutional role.\nKowloon's response to the cohorts' record-destruction pattern: all crisis operations include a standing obligation to preserve administrative records even during evacuation.\nRecords travel first. Personnel travel second. Equipment travels third. A unit can be reconstituted; records cannot be reconstructed from memory.\nThe Black Ledger notes: all but two of the named Oathbroken Cohorts destroyed records. The two that did not are the only ones whose violations could be documented and adjudicated — the strongest argument for record preservation as the primary custody obligation.\nKowloon views civilian abandonment as less damaging than record destruction in long-term institutional damage; the prioritization is controversial but explicitly explained in internal training materials.
