577	71	1	0	The Cistern Ring was the oldest known hydraulic regulation system in the world, built by the\nKeepers of Measure at the Great Oasis during the early road network era. It comprised four\nunderground catchment chambers arranged in a ring below the oasis basin: the North Cistern,\nshallowest, for daily distribution; the East Cistern, deepest, reserved for drought emergency;\nthe South Cistern, allocated to caravan provisioning; and the West Cistern, allocated to\nagricultural irrigation of the oasis gardens. The ring was not designed to store water—the\nnatural springs provided that—but to regulate flow. During flood season, excess water was\ndiverted into the chambers to prevent basin overflow and road erosion. During drought, stored\nwater was released through calibrated sluice gates to maintain minimum basin levels. Each\nchamber had its own sluice master; the four sluice masters together formed the Water Court,\nthe earliest governing body at the oasis, predating any royal authority by several generations.\nThe Cistern Ring became the template for every subsequent cistern built along the eastern road\nnetwork. Its construction methods, chamber geometry, and sluice calibration standards were\ncopied by Keeper engineers across the desert corridor.
