583	71	7	2052	Deltari Reed Law treats hydraulic systems as witnessed measurement problems: flow rates, sluice conditions, and water-debt ledgers must all be independently confirmed and regularly re-measured because conditions change.\nThe Cistern Ring's original calibration standards required quarterly sluice inspections with co-signature from all four masters — no single master's reading was accepted as authoritative without at least one independent confirmation. This is the earliest documented application of Deltari measurement principles to hydraulic governance.\nThe Ring's collapse came after governance fracture eliminated the co-inspection requirement, not after physical failure of the engineering itself.\nThe lesson Deltari draw: hydraulic infrastructure outlasts the measurement protocols that maintain it, and when measurement protocols collapse, engineering follows within generations.
584	71	8	16388	Kethari water-memory preserves knowledge of the Cistern Ring from before the Water Court era — not as institutional history but as ecological observation of the oasis basin itself: the flood-season overflow patterns, the drought-season spring diminishment cycles, the way the basin's edges changed over time.\nThe Ring's engineering modified these patterns; Kethari water-memory preserved the pre-engineering baseline.\nWhen the Ring's collapse allowed the basin to contract toward its unregulated state, Kethari observations provided the only existing account of what that state looked like — information the Spring Wardens who now manage the surviving north cistern chamber have consulted when projecting long-term basin behavior.\nKethari do not evaluate the Cistern Ring as an institution; they evaluate it as a modification to an aquifer they have known longer than the institution existed.
