628	77	1	0	The Speaking Vent is a Western Fold vent feature in the Cinderteeth Mountains known for pulsed gas emissions that produce audible resonance patterns.\nLocated in the volcanic terrain of the Western Fold — the same zone that housed the Spirebound Conclave's primary sanctum before the collapse — the vent emits through a narrow basalt fissure approximately two meters wide and indeterminate depth.\nThe emissions are not continuous but pulsed at irregular intervals, producing pressure waves that the surrounding rock amplifies into audible low-frequency tones.\nVentspeaker practice — an older interpretive tradition practiced by communities living on the Cinderteeth's lower slopes — held that the vent's pulse patterns constituted patterned warnings about seismic activity, eruption timing, and weather.\nThe Conclave's Ember Canon faction documented the vent extensively, treating it as a heat-signature data source rather than a voice.\nAfter the Conclave's collapse, the vent's interpretive community contracted severely; few practitioners of formal Ventspeaker discipline remain.\nRangers operating from Kiess staging areas who travel into the Cinderteeth include the Speaking Vent's current activity status in their reports as a seismic indicator.\nThe vent's pulse timing has been observed to change in the days preceding significant eruption events, lending retrospective credibility to the Ventspeaker interpretive tradition.\nModern assessors treat the vent as a natural geological feature with useful predictive properties rather than as a communicating entity.\nThe Conclave's sealed Ember Canon records from Zone C of the sanctum include the most complete emissions log of the Speaking Vent in existence — inaccessible until those records are recovered.\nThe vent's sound is described by those who have heard it as bone-deep rather than ear-audible, felt through the feet and chest before it is heard.
