786	97	3	4	The Vigil was designed by the founding houses to make institutional memory physically embodied: darkness is not symbolic but structural. During the Vigil hour, Kowloon's gates are locked open — enacting the Neon Covenant's principle that shelter during disaster must be unconditional. The jade seal's two-magistrate requirement during the Vigil is the most explicit architectural feature of the Covenant's distributed-authority principle: even in commemoration, no single official holds full power. The Black Ledger's sealed section was argued as a necessary protection during the third Vigil, when testimony deposited in the first two had become politically actionable against sitting magistrates. The Courier Lantern Office maintains a separate Vigil register of persons identified posthumously from Yen-Mak's population. The founding text states: "Let darkness be the ledger. Let each name read be an entry. Let no office be so urgent it cannot wait one hour for the dead."
