885	109	1	0	The Keepers of Measure were the earliest civilization to leave functional records — a pre-urban governance network that ruled through calibration rather than armies.\nThey governed road networks, cistern systems, and caravan law across the eastern desert and coastal territories through standardized measurement: road stones set to star angles, cistern basins graded by seasonal drift, caravan levies assessed in water rights.\nTheir writing system, Measure Script, encoded direction, volume, legal status, and time in single glyphs — each inscription was simultaneously a description and a legal instrument.\nTo inscribe was to adjudicate: Measure Script had no purely descriptive mode, only a mode where description and legal obligation were the same act.\nThe Keepers developed two major branches of their writing system: the inland branch (managing caravan law, road maintenance, and cistern systems) and the Littoral Branch (managing coastal and maritime contexts).\nKowloon's Jade Magistracy notation and Mafdet's Ledger Houses both descend from the Littoral Branch — the coastal trade document tradition carried by maritime routes rather than overland caravans.\nThe inland branch produced the Measure Script inscription traditions that survive in the Great Oasis's Waystone Circle, the charter stone system, and the Scorching Sands' obelisk-line timing method.\nThe Keepers' road network formalized the Great Oasis as the administrative calibration node; survey crews set shadow rods against the basin's central gnomon stone, and all measurements across the network were referenced back to this point.\nThe Keepers formally documented non-human communities — Khephari, Kethari, Serathi, Deltari — in their survey records as corridor peoples with specialized knowledge, but assigned them no institutional standing in the governance system.\nTheir downfall was structural: the Keepers governed through calibration and could not adapt when the Salt Ledger Kingdoms converted road-tax