1021	125	1	0	The Lantern Reforms were Midgaard's second major governance layer, added during the Standardization Century when population growth outpaced the First Stone Accord's militia-and-tribunal framework.\nNamed for the lamp-lines system — standardized oil lanterns posted at regular intervals along primary corridors and watched-square perimeters — the Reforms made Midgaard's governance physically legible to strangers.\nThe core innovations were: standardized paving marks (symbols identifying road type and jurisdiction), posted tariffs (fixed rates displayed at trade entry points), district lamp-lines (the lantern network defining where civic obligation applied), and rotating night patrol routes tied to lantern station intervals.\nEach lantern station created an administrative node: a posted notice point, a patrol rendezvous, and a visible marker of where watched-square security extended.\nThe Reforms were driven by a practical problem: merchants from multiple origins needed to navigate Midgaard's dispute system, and the system was not legible to people who did not already know its oral traditions.\nStandardization was security infrastructure — merchants who could not read the system could not comply with it, and non-compliance created friction that the militia system had to resolve through force rather than procedure.\nThe Lantern Reforms' lamp-lines are the origin of the folk belief that "Midgaard never stops watching" — the city that maintains visible light through every night as a continuous assertion of civic order.\nThe Reforms introduced the district concept: defined territories with specific administrative oversight, distinct from the earlier ward-captain system's informal boundaries.\nDistrict boundaries were set to correspond to existing commercial patterns rather than geographic features, which is why Midgaard's district map looks arbitrary from the outside but makes sense from the merchant's perspective.\nThe lamp-lines system was also the first infrastructure capable of transmitting emergency signals: lanterns unlit, lit in sequence, or lit in specific combinations communicated gate-closure, fire-alarm, or tribunal-assembly calls across the city without requiring messengers.\nThe Lantern Reforms' physical infrastructure — the lamp-lines, paving marks, and posted notice boards — survived into every subsequent governance layer and are still visible in Midgaard's street design.
1030	126	3	4	Kowloon's Neon Covenant reads the Strandline Compact with recognition: both documents are responses to institutional collapse, both prioritize operational continuity over governance elegance.\nThe "one ledger, one ration code" principle of the Neon Covenant echoes the Compact's "dual attestation" principle — both reflect the conviction that documentary precision is the foundation of civic trust.\nKowloon's Jade Magistracy negotiates trade agreements through all three Compact blocs simultaneously, because any one bloc can disrupt the others.\nThe Compact's structure prevents Kowloon from simply buying trade access to Mafdet through financial relationships with the Ledger Houses — Harbor Wardens can still restrict quay access, and the Guild can redirect overland routes.
1022	125	2	1	The Reforms codified commercial law (tariff compliance, contract registration) and civic law (militia obligation, tribunal attendance) as separate but overlapping systems in different offices. Before the Reforms, ward captains served as both commercial arbiters and militia commanders — concentrated authority producing inconsistent outcomes. The split created Midgaard's three-office structure — complex but mutually reviewable — credited by the Continuity faction as the governance layer that made Midgaard stable enough to persist through crises. The Reckoning faction notes that the Reforms' district boundaries, combined with the Violet Compact's registry-primacy principle, created the infrastructure for identifying unregistered persons — the eventual condemned-debtor transfer mechanism. The lamp-lines' emergency signaling was repurposed during the Ash-and-Iron period as a population-tracking alert system: specific lantern sequences indicated which districts had unregistered persons requiring intake processing.
1023	125	3	4	Kowloon's Neon Covenant color system was partly influenced by Midgaard's Lantern Reforms, though Kowloon developed the concept significantly further. The Reforms used lanterns as administrative nodes and emergency signals; Kowloon's color-coding extended the same principle into every surface material, creating a city readable at a glance rather than only at posted notices. Kowloon's specific colors were drawn from the delta's existing material culture rather than from Midgaard's lamp-oil yellow standard. The Courier Lantern Office descended from the Lampkeeper tradition that predated even the Neon Covenant; the Reforms' lamp-line concept arrived in Kowloon as something the city was already doing in more sophisticated form. Kowloon views the Lantern Reforms as Midgaard's first serious attempt at governance legibility, and considers Midgaard's system still less readable than Kowloon's.
