1096	134	1	0	The Iron Compact Appeal was Rakuen's third and most formally prepared annexation petition, submitted eleven years ago during the Modern Tension era.\nIt differed from previous petitions in institutional preparation: Kiess had certified its legal standing through the inter-city petition review framework, and Mafdet had co-signed as a supporting city.\nThe petition proposed staged legal integration — a phased annexation schedule designed to avoid sudden administrative shock to either city's systems.\nMidgaard refused on constitutional charter-sovereignty grounds: the Charter Sovereignty office held that Midgaard's founding compact did not provide a mechanism for sovereign incorporation of an already-chartered independent city.\nThe refusal created the most politically complicated outcome of the three petition cycles: Kiess's certification made the refusal a direct confrontation with Kiess's legal authority, not just Rakuen's petition.\nMafdet's co-signature meant the refusal was a five-city political event rather than a bilateral Midgaard-Rakuen decision.\nThe response to the refusal was indirect compensatory mechanisms: Kowloon's Southern Grain Compact formalization, Kiess's standing charter-consultation offer, and Mafdet's expanded mercy-freight classification, none of which provided annexation.\nThree factions emerged in Rakuen's political aftermath: the Amendment Track (seeking to modify Midgaard's charter to create an annexation pathway), the Quiet Separatists (developing maritime independence through Mafdet), and the Preservation Coalition (maintaining current inter-city cooperation without annexation).\nThe Iron Compact Appeal's petition text is the most legally dense of the three: it cites First Stone Accord language, Kiess's anti-sealed-warrant precedent, Kowloon's Southern Grain Compact obligations, and Mafdet's maritime cargo law in a unified argument that Rakuen's integration serves the five-city network's founding purpose.\nMidgaard's charter