100	5705	advocate's charter sash	The Violet Compact's Midgaard allies issue this sash to those who disrupt active testimony-destruction operations inside the cathedral. It is not merely decorative â the certification stamps are functional registry marks that grant the wearer provisional advocate status in any Compact-recognized proceeding. In Midgaard's civic courts, a person wearing this sash may submit recovered testimony without a sponsoring clerk, bypassing the usual institutional bottleneck.\n
101	5706	recovered relic manifest	The manifest documents reliquary holdings that were removed from Compact custody during the cathedral's institutional withdrawal, items that were civic archive material misappropriated into ecclesiastical holdings. Midgaard's relic adjudication offices recognize this manifest as a recovery instrument: presenting it initiates a formal restitution proceeding. The items it documents include at least three classified as "missing under Unindexed Years conditions" in Midgaard's own ledgers.\n
102	5707	unsealed testimony binding	When a null-seal archivist's seal is physically removed from a suppressed record, the document's legal admissibility is technically restored under the Compact's original continuity clauses. Midgaard's Reckoning Faction has argued for years that the null-sealing process was applied to material that should never have been suppressed. This binding's contents â now legally admissible â appear to support that position. The Reckoning Faction would consider this artifact extremely valuable.\n
103	5708	thorn reformist's mantle	This mantle was worn by clergymen within the Thorned Scriptorium who attempted internal reform of the Cardinal's architectural coercion systems. That they tried, failed, and continued to wear this mantle as visible dissent is documented in the wing's own annotation halls â a documentation decision Seredh made deliberately, on the theory that visible, contained dissent is safer than dissent that goes underground. He may have been right. The reformists are dead. The records of their failure are intact.\n
104	5709	Vos Thane's registration ledger page	This is one of the pages Registrar Vos Thane attempted to forward to the Magistrate Ledgerhouse before the Silenced Vespers. It documents a systematic discrepancy: high-born petitioners receiving absolution for the same offenses that produced condemnation sentences for bonded laborers. The evidence is procedurally meticulous â Thane was a careful archivist. He knew this page needed to withstand institutional challenge. Whether it ever reached a neutral court is the question Midgaard's Reckoning Faction is still trying to answer.\n
105	5710	Sister Caelen's ash-margin codex	The surface text is liturgical boilerplate. The marginalia â written in ash-shorthand that mimics liturgical annotation â are pre-withdrawal records that Caelen preserved by hiding them inside the binding of a manuscript too sacred to destroy. Vharos identified her system and had her notes sealed but kept the codex as reference material because the records it preserved were too useful to suppress entirely. He left a note in his own hand on the inside cover: "Kept for operational continuity. Do not distribute."\n
106	5711	Bell-Warden Oris's silenced clapper	This is the clapper Bell-Warden Oris removed when he ordered the bell-lines silenced during the Silenced Vespers. He kept it. His name appears on the Shattered Rose Gate's dedication stone, crossed out in obsidian lacquer â the first person eclipsed by the nascent doctrine, a man who obeyed the order that enabled his own erasure. The cathedral preserved the clapper as institutional evidence that the Silenced Vespers was an ordered event. Whether this constitutes a confession or a trophy is a matter the institution has not resolved.\n
107	5712	defaced ward-threshold stone	Beneath the obsidian overpainting, the stone's original registry marks are still legible in profile: the compass-rose mark of the Lantern Registry, a ward designation number, and the Compact oversight notation "Western Ward Threshold â Compact Clearance Required." The stone was not destroyed. It was painted over. The cathedral kept it because destroying it would have removed evidence of the original civic authority that granted the chapel-house its initial operating charter â a charter the cathedral still technically cites when defending its institutional legitimacy.\n
108	5713	transit ledger for sealed warrants	The ledger documents the chapel-house's role in processing sealed warrants â the administrative instruments by which Midgaard condemned debtors, petty criminals, and unregistered persons to labor service, reclassifying them from citizens to arcane material for transfer to the Spirebound Conclave. The chapel-house did not originate these warrants. It processed them. The ledger documents exactly how. This is among the records Midgaard's Reckoning Faction has sought for generations.\n
109	5714	eclipse withdrawal charter	The document that ended Midgaard's civic oversight of the chapel-house. Its language is precise and deliberately opaque: "withdrawn into eclipse until judgment ripens." No Midgaard magistrate challenged it. The Continuity Faction's archives list this charter as "under institutional review" â a status it has held for longer than most political careers. The Reckoning Faction calls it "the document that licensed the entire archive." Both assessments are accurate.\n
110	5715	original Compact threshold charter	The founding document of the Violet Arch Chapel-House, granting it authority to conduct threshold adjudication, ritual quarantine, and archive relay for the Compact. The three annotating hands belong to Noctyra (who added color-rank liturgical gloss), Vharos (who added null-seal procedural amendments), and a third unidentified hand that wrote only "this was not what was authorized" in the margin beside the blood-tithe protocols. The third hand is not dated.\n
111	5716	choir polyphony scroll	The scroll presents Ilyra's polyphonic confession system as a musical document â voice parts, timing marks, procedural annotations â in a notation system that would be readable to any trained musician as performance instruction. Only context reveals that "subject entrance" means the person being processed, that "yield marks" indicate blood-collection timing, and that "seal confirmation" is the moment testimony becomes institutionally binding. The scroll was designed to be legible as worship. It functions as an operations manual.\n
112	5717	obsidian tri-sign witness lock	The lock cycles through three simultaneous requirements â a geometric pattern (crescent), an acoustic signal (bell), and a visual obstruction (veil) â ensuring that no single person can access secured material alone. Vharos imported the design from the Gloamvault's Sepulcher Descent. Its inscription: "Three signs open; consensus unlocks; solitary effort fails. This is not security. This is governance." The lock protects not against intrusion but against individual unilateral action. The collective requirement is the security model.\n
113	5718	moonbone mortification record	The record lists hundreds of entries. Each entry: a name, a date of death, a disposition code (R, A, or Q), and â for Q entries â a utilization notation indicating how the Requisitioned person's remains and records were incorporated into institutional use. The Q entries outnumber the R and A entries by approximately forty to one. A note at the front in the Hierophant's hand: "Requisition is not punishment. It is the highest form of institutional continuity."\n
114	5719	eclipse high nave keystone	The keystone was the architectural capstone of the cathedral's central nave before it fell. The eclipse symbol carved into it â full disc, crescent shadow, the founding image of the institution's doctrine â was designed to be readable from ground level only in certain light conditions, when shadows cast by the rose windows fell at the correct angle. In that light, the keystone's shadow would project the eclipse symbol onto the nave floor, marking the exact center of the cathedral's spatial authority. The keystone no longer hangs above anything. The shadow no longer falls.\n
