140	2214	engineering inscriptions	The wall documentation covers the complete design specification of the cistern ring: chamber volumes, flow rates, sluice gate dimensions, maintenance schedules, and emergency procedures. The emergency procedures section, covering six walls, details responses to drought, flood, earthquake, siege, and what the Keepers called civil disruption â a category that the Battle of the Dry Basin apparently qualified as, given the damage. The margin of the civil disruption section carries a handwritten note: this procedure was not sufficient.\n
141	2218	salt tablets	Hundreds of salt tablets fill the vault's primary racks, each tablet recording a water-right transaction, charter registration, or administrative decision of the High Water Court. The tablets are organized by year and case number in a filing system that the vault's own indexing documents explain. The most recent tablets are dated approximately three generations ago â the last period of active Water Court administration before the Battle of the Dry Basin.\n
142	2218	ledger volumes	The water-debt ledger volumes record every water draw from the oasis basin and cistern ring for over a century of Water Court administration. Each volume covers one year and contains entries for every caravan and resident draw. The final volume ends mid-year with a note in a different hand: court suspended pending resolution of dry basin dispute. The resolution never came.\n
143	2225	final inscription	Below the last flow record, someone carved: the cistern engineers of the west quarter, who maintained this chamber from its first season to its last, record here that we did our work correctly. The drought did not come from any failure of this chamber. The Ninth Meridian Rite was not our decision. This chamber was built well and may be restored when the water returns. The inscription is in the Keeper's most formal engineering script.\n
144	2234	archive tubes	The surviving ceramic tubes are sealed with wax and bronze clamps that have resisted the water. Their label inscriptions, visible through the clear water, identify the contents as secondary copies of astronomical observation records â the Keepers' sky-watching data from three generations of observations. Several tubes are cracked at their seals and their contents are certainly destroyed. The undamaged tubes could be retrieved and their contents read, if someone was willing to dive for them.\n
145	2244	spring vent	The limestone spring vent is approximately a forearm's diameter at its surface emergence, widening as it descends into the fractured rock below. The water emerging from it is measurably colder than the surrounding aquifer water and carries a different mineral signature â geologists who have studied similar vents elsewhere describe this as evidence of a different aquifer layer, deeper and older than the primary cistern supply. This spring has been flowing since before the Keepers surveyed the oasis. It has never gone dry in recorded history.\n
146	2248	deep pool water	Looking straight down through the pool's water column reveals layers: clear water to twenty feet, then a haze of fine mineral particulate at the thermocline, then clearer again below to the bottom at forty feet where the spring vent's upwelling stirs a slow vortex across the deepest sediment. The vent at the bottom is large enough to accept a person's head and shoulders. Bubbles from whatever lies beyond the vent rise continuously through the water column in a thin stream that disperses before reaching the surface.\n
147	2249	wyrm scales	Up close, the Wyrm's scales are the most complex natural material in the oasis â each one a layered composite of the creature's own biological material and the aquifer's dissolved minerals, built up over decades of deep-water habitation. The scale color shifts between angles: blue-grey in direct light, copper-green in phosphorescent light, nearly black in shadow. The largest scales at the creature's mid-body are the size of a buckler shield and harder than cut limestone.\n
148	2249	spring vents	The three primary spring vents in the lair floor are the original sources of the Deepwell Confluence â the springs whose existence made the Great Oasis possible before human civilization arrived to use them. The vents flow at a rate that has remained consistent for as long as records have been kept, and their output feeds everything above: the basin, the cistern ring, the garden terraces, the wells in the warden settlement. The Wyrm's body partially seals two of the three vents, but the pressure is sufficient that the water forces its way through regardless.\n
