997	7175	gas inversion detection protocol	The commission's detection protocol for vent-gas inversion pockets was developed after the first unwarned encounter with this site, in which a commission team member experienced significant dizziness before retreating.  The protocol: approach any depression in Crown terrain with one lead person extended arm-length ahead of the group; if the lead person notices any sweet-sulfur odor, all personnel stop and the lead person retreats; test-drop a handful of scree fragments into the depression and observe their behavior (gas concentration causes slight condensation visible on cold fragments in the right lighting); stake the pocket rim for future reference.  The protocol's requirement for a lead person ahead of the group reflects the fact that the safety margin between first detection and dangerous concentration is approximately one meter at still-air conditions and depends entirely on wind direction and speed — in crosswind conditions the pocket may be safe; in still conditions it must be treated as an active hazard.\n
998	7176	pocket beta death cases	The three confirmed extraction crew deaths at Pocket Beta occurred in two separate incidents over the space of four seasons.  The first incident: a three-person crew in the process of retreating from a nearby extraction site, moving quickly in reduced visibility, entered the pocket before the lead person detected the odor; two members of the crew collapsed before the third could extract them, and the extraction attempt brought the third within the lethal concentration zone.  All three were recovered from the pocket rim by a rescue party working from windward.  The second incident: a lone extraction worker, violating the partner protocol, entered the pocket in an apparent attempt to shortcut across the depression; their remains were found at the pocket floor.  The commission's survey report describes the pocket beta fatalities as the direct cause of the licensing system's partner requirement and the detailed evacuation protocol signage installed throughout the glasswalk zone.\n
999	7180	summit sightline catalog	The commission's sightline catalog from the Widow's Spire summit documented eleven named reference points visible in favorable conditions.  Looking north: the northern ridge arm of the Cinderteeth, the GNF Northwestern Crown treeline, and the distant suggestion of the higher GNF interior.  Looking east: the northeastern ridge system connecting to the GNF border, the three GNF linking ridges (from which the survey team could see smoke from the GNF's northern ranger station on clear days).  Looking south: the Glasswash Fan scarp in the middle distance, the Forest of Confusion visible in the far south as a grey-green haze, and on exceptional days the distant geometry of the desert regions south of the forest.  Looking west: the saddle pass approaches, the lower foothills, and on the clearest days the boundary markers of the Roc Road route that the Kiess expeditions used.  The catalog provides a comprehensive orientation for anyone reaching the summit and constitutes one of the most practical pieces of survey documentation the commission produced.\n
1000	7187	lightning-grounded ash column discharge	The phenomenon of lightning grounding through the ash column to surface metal was documented in three commission incidents during the Crown survey, all involving equipment rather than personnel due to the commission's strict low-profile protocol compliance.  The largest discharge event destroyed a metal tripod and its mounted instrument, the discharge traveling down the tripod from the instrument head to the ground anchor, volatilizing the instrument housing and leaving a characteristic carbon trace on the rock surface at the base.  The commission's analysis found that the ash column's charge buildup was predictable with sufficient observation time — specific visual characteristics of column density and structure predicted elevated discharge risk — and that the observation-based prediction protocol reduced the surprise element sufficiently to allow planned equipment stowage rather than reactive emergency stowage.  The commission's lightning protocol was adopted in modified form by the Kiess extraction licensing system for all Crown operations.\n
1001	7188	glassfall characteristics	Glassfall is the product of a specific eruption mechanism: lava fountaining that sends molten droplets into the cold air above the vent, where they solidify into glass particles whose size and shape depend on the droplet's trajectory and cooling rate.  Large droplets from high-energy events produce the lenticular glass pieces found in the debris field — centimeter-scale, lens-shaped, with smooth curved faces and sharp edges where they fractured on landing or subsequent thermal cycling.  Small droplets produce the fine glass dust that coats the glassfall zone surface and creates the cutting hazard for exposed surfaces.  The commission's geological analysis confirmed that the glassfall debris distribution documents the Firsttooth's eruption history in the surrounding terrain, with different chemical compositions in different layers corresponding to different eruptive periods.\n
1002	7232	ferric scoria formation	Ferric scoria is distinguished from ordinary basalt scoria by its characteristic rust-orange to deep red coloration, the product of iron oxidation at high temperature during rapid cooling.  The specific color of ferric scoria indicates the oxidation state of the iron at the time of quenching: bright orange indicates full oxidation to ferric iron compounds, dark red indicates partial oxidation with mixed ferrous-ferric states, and the black streaks visible through some samples indicate iron sulfide formation where sulfur-rich gases were incorporated during solidification.  The commission's mineral classification identified the Firsttooth's mid-caldera scoria as unusually uniform in composition, suggesting a consistent lava chemistry across the most recent eruptive cycle that produced this surface layer.\n
1003	7239	name-stone carvings	The name-stone carvings at the Oracle Vent represent a geological-archaeological record spanning multiple centuries of Ash Cult pilgrimage activity: the oldest legible stones have weathered dates that pre-date the current doctrinal framework, suggesting the site has been a pilgrimage focus since before the Cult's formal organizational period.  The carving style evolved across time periods: early stones use a simple incised-line script that the commission's epigraphic observer identified as a regional variant of a broader highland notation system; middle-period stones show a more elaborate interlaced design incorporating doctrinal symbols; recent stones range from the highly skilled (elaborate multi-register carvings with pictographic and textual elements integrated) to the functionally minimal (a name and date only, deeply incised for permanence).\n
1004	7239	offering accumulation	The offerings at the Oracle Vent lip are classified in commission records into categories by material: mineral offerings (carefully selected volcanic glass, scoria samples, obsidian pieces), organic offerings (preserved botanical material from the lower zones, now desiccated in the upper-altitude dry air), and manufactured offerings (carved objects, woven cords, metal items of Ash Cult devotional craft).  The arrangement protocol observed by the commission's cultural observer places mineral offerings closest to the vent lip, organic offerings in the middle ring, and manufactured offerings at the outer edge — a classification that the commission observer interpreted as reflecting Cult doctrine about the hierarchy of geological, biological, and cultural phenomena.\n
1005	7247	sanctum gate	The sanctum gate is a geological arch whose natural form has been worked by craftspeople whose tools and methods the commission's archaeological observer could not confidently identify: the arch's natural basalt has been dressed to precise tolerances, its edges shaped with a smoothness inconsistent with the thermal-modification tools documented in Ash Cult material culture.  The commission observer's notes suggest that the gate's working predates the current Ash Cult organization by several organizational generations — that whoever shaped it was not the current Cult but an earlier culture that occupied the Ember Crown at a period the current Cult does not document.  The keystone of the arch bears a carved inscription in a script the commission observer could not match to any documented regional writing system.\n
1006	7248	how different civilizations interpreted the Throne	The commission's cultural survey compiled documented accounts of the Ember Throne from eight distinct cultural traditions that have reached this point over the recorded period.  All eight accounts independently describe the Throne as a seat of geological intelligence: something that perceives, considers, and delivers verdicts according to criteria that none of the accounts agree on but all consider real.  The disagreements between cultural accounts are instructive: the Ash Cult holds that the Throne judges the living; an older account from the commission's archive describes it as judging geological time; the earliest legible account from the antechamber walls describes it as "the accumulation of all the mountain's memories."  The commission observer's notes conclude with the observation that the physical Throne formation is unambiguously natural in origin — no human agency could produce a structure of this geological character — but that the cultures who encountered it have attributed agency to it across independent traditions in a way that the observer found scientifically puzzling.\n
1008	7251	summit pumice quality	The differentiation between summit pumice and lower-zone pumice depends on the eruption event's characteristics: summit pumice derives from the highest-energy events where magma is fragmented under extreme pressure at the vent and ejected with sufficient force to cool rapidly in the upper air column.  The resulting vesicle structure is fine-grained and uniform, with bubble diameters averaging two to three millimeters and total porosity in the seventy to eighty percent range — significantly higher than the forty to sixty percent typical of lower-zone material.  The commission's sample set from this field was classified as premium filtration grade, suitable for precision filtration applications requiring consistent pore characteristics.\n
1009	7252	sulfur crystal quality	Sulfur crystal quality for alchemical purposes depends on purity, crystal size, and formation context.  The Summit Bloom deposit's crystals are remarkable on all three metrics: the fumarolic condensation mechanism that deposits them produces high-purity sulfur by a natural fractional distillation process, with impurities remaining in the vapor phase while pure sulfur condenses on cooler rock surfaces at the deposit elevation.  Crystal size in this deposit ranges from centimeter-scale individual formations up to multi-centimeter clusters where undisturbed growth has continued over multiple deposition cycles.\n
1010	7253	origin-authentication by color and bubble structure	Mirror-quality Cinderteeth obsidian is authenticated by two characteristics observable without laboratory analysis: the distinctive greenish cross-section tint and the near-total absence of macroscopic bubbles.  The greenish tint is produced by trace amounts of dissolved volcanic gases — primarily chlorine compounds — incorporated at a specific concentration range during the cooling event: too little produces clear glass, too much produces a turbid material with reduced reflective quality, and the specific range that produces the mirror-grade material is characteristic of this seam's lava chemistry.  The absence of visible bubbles in mirror-grade material is the practical authentication marker used by traders: a bubble density of fewer than one per square centimeter of cross-section is the threshold for mirror-grade classification, and authentic Cinderteeth summit obsidian from this seam consistently achieves densities below one per ten square centimeters.\n
